faq:
  - q: "How do you answer 'why do you want to work here'?"
    a: "Three components: (1) Something specific about this company — a product decision, a recent announcement, a technical approach, a business model. Not 'I've heard great things.' (2) A connection between that specific thing and your own background or genuine interest — why it resonates with you specifically. (3) A forward-looking statement — what you would contribute or build in this role. Generic answers ('I'm excited about the opportunity to grow') fail immediately. Specific answers that reference a real insight convert."
  - q: "What are interviewers evaluating with 'why do you want to work here'?"
    a: "Three things: (1) Did you do real research — or are you recycling a generic answer? (2) Do you have genuine motivation beyond the paycheck? (3) Is your motivation aligned with what this role and company actually offer? Interviewers can distinguish a prepared-specific answer from a recycled-generic one within the first sentence."
  - q: "What should you research before answering this question?"
    a: "Five sources: (1) The company's recent news, announcements, and press releases. (2) The specific product or engineering problem you would work on. (3) The company's engineering or design blog and public technical content. (4) Recent LinkedIn posts or conference talks from the hiring manager or team leads. (5) What differentiates this company from its most obvious competitors. One specific insight from any of these produces a non-generic answer."
  - q: "How specific does 'why do you want to work here' need to be?"
    a: "Specific enough that the same answer could not be given to a different company. 'I admire your culture of innovation' could be said to any company. 'I've been following your migration from a monolithic Rails app to a service-based architecture — it maps directly to what I led at [Company] and I want to go deeper in that direction' cannot be said to anyone else."
  - q: "What if you are interviewing at multiple companies and don't have a strong preference?"
    a: "Answer as if this company is your preference — because in this interview, it should be. The answer does not require you to have only applied here. It requires you to give a genuine, specific reason for this particular company. Every company in your final round deserves that level of preparation. Find something real to be interested in — not because you are being deceptive, but because genuine interest is findable at most good companies if you look."
